<header><p class="st-kicker">other · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Frisian Freedom</h1><p class="st-meta">1100 CE – 1498 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A medieval coastal society without lords, in which Frisian farmers governed themselves through elected judges and assemblies for nearly four centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the Carolingian counts lost effective control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> coast in the 11th and 12th centuries, the Frisian lands between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlie">Vlie</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weser">Weser</a> governed themselves. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/frisii">Frisians</a> claimed their liberty came from a privilege granted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>, a legend that justified their refusal to accept feudal lords.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Each land or community chose its own judges, called redjeven or consuls, and delegates of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-sealands">Seven Sealands</a> met at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upstalsboom">Upstalsboom</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurich">Aurich</a>. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/frisii">Frisians</a> built dykes and terps, raised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and traded by sea, and wrote down their own laws in Old Frisian. There were no knights or castles in the usual sense, but powerful chieftains, the hoofdelingen, rose in the 14th century.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Feuds between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schieringers">Schieringers</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vetkopers">Vetkopers</a> weakened the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/frisii">Frisians</a> in the 15th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-frisia">East Frisia</a> came under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-cirksena">Cirksena</a> counts in 1464, and in 1498 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximilian-i-holy-roman-emperor">Emperor Maximilian</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-iii-duke-of-saxony">Albert of Saxony</a> governor of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-frisia">Friesland</a>, ending the freedom west of the Ems and creating the Lordship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frisia">Frisia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/frisian-freedom.jpg" alt="Frisian Freedom" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sigillum_iudicum_Selandiarum_Frisiae_Transparent_2.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1100 CE – 1498 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Frisian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Netherlands, Germany</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Friesland, Seven Frisian Sealands</dd></dl></aside></div>
